<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Adolf Pengel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jopie Pengel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Adolf Pengel was a Surinamese politician who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-of-suriname">National Party of Suriname</a> (NPS), the main party of the Afro-Surinamese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> population. He was prime minister from 1963 to 1969, governing in coalition with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagernath-lachmon">Jagernath Lachmon</a>'s Hindustani party, a partnership known as the politics of fraternisation. His government fell in 1969 after strikes by teachers and other workers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-adolf-pengel.jpg" alt="Johan Adolf Pengel" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jack de Nijs for Anefo / Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minister-President_Pengel_profil_1963_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pengel was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> on 20 January 1916. He studied law but did not graduate. When a landowner named Van Dijck, who rented a large parcel of ground near Pengel's home to poor Afro and Indo-Surinamese tenants, began to demand large rent increases, Pengel started a protest movement against him and raised enough money to buy the land. In 1949 he joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-of-suriname">National Party of Suriname</a> and was elected to the Estates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, and he soon became one of the most influential politicians in the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1952 he became chairman of the General Alliance of Labour Unions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, and in 1955 he was elected chairman of the Estates. That year the NPS under Pengel and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/progressive-reform-party-suriname">United Hindustani Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagernath-lachmon">Jagernath Lachmon</a> formed the coalition that took power in 1958 and allowed the two communities to understand each other. In 1958 Pengel was formateur of the Emanuels government but chose to remain party leader rather than join it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Pengel became prime minister on 30 June 1963. His government built roads, greatly improved the existing infrastructure and opened two new hospitals. Re-elected in 1967, he also served as minister of general affairs, the interior and finance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/states-general-of-the-netherlands">States General of the Netherlands</a> judged that his government was spending too much and cut development aid, which set off the strikes that brought it down on 5 March 1969.</p><p>He died of sepsis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> on 5 June 1970, aged 54. His country residence on the Blackwatra Creek at Carolina, where artificial rapids had been built, became the Blaka Watra recreation area, and the lions he received in 1966 led to the Paramaribo Zoo, opened in 1972. A statue by Stuart Robles de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> was unveiled on Onafhankelijkheidsplein in 1974, and in 1990 the airport at Zanderij was renamed Johan Adolf Pengel International Airport.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johan Adolf Pengel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Charter for the Kingdom of the Netherlands of 1954 made Suriname an autonomous country within the kingdom, with its own government and parliament, in which parties largely followed ethnic lines, such as the Creole-based NPS of <b>Johan Adolf Pengel</b> and the Hindustani-based VHP of Jagernath Lachmon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=5.87,-55.17&amp;year=1951" aria-label="Paramaribo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johan Adolf Pengel"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5411 4499h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5411 4499h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=5.87,-55.17&amp;year=1951">What was here in 1951? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
